# Conjugacy Classes of $GL(n, \mathbb{R})$

## Setup

Two elements $A, B \in GL(n, \mathbb{R})$ are **conjugate** in $GL(n, \mathbb{R})$ iff there exists $P \in GL(n, \mathbb{R})$ with $B = P A P^{-1}$. This is precisely the notion of **similarity of real $n \times n$ matrices**. Hence determining the conjugacy classes of $GL(n, \mathbb{R})$ is equivalent to classifying invertible real $n \times n$ matrices up to real similarity. The classification is given by the **real Jordan canonical form**.

---

## The Real Jordan Canonical Form

### Complex eigenvalues and real structure

Let $A \in M_n(\mathbb{R})$. Viewed as a complex matrix, $A$ has eigenvalues in $\mathbb{C}$. Since $A$ has real entries, the non-real eigenvalues occur in conjugate pairs $\lambda = a+bi$, $\bar\lambda = a-bi$ with $a \in \mathbb{R}$, $b > 0$. The real eigenvalues lie in $\mathbb{R}$.

Over $\mathbb{C}$, $A$ is similar to its Jordan form: a block-diagonal matrix with Jordan blocks $J_k(\lambda) = \lambda I_k + N_k$, where $N_k$ has $1$'s on the superdiagonal and $0$'s elsewhere. The pair $(\lambda, k)$ for each block, together with multiplicities, is a complete invariant over $\mathbb{C}$.

### Passing to real blocks

For a **real eigenvalue** $\lambda \in \mathbb{R}$, the Jordan block $J_k(\lambda)$ is already a real $k \times k$ matrix, and it appears unchanged in the real form.

For a **complex conjugate pair** $a \pm bi$ ($b > 0$), a Jordan block $J_k(\lambda)$ of size $k$ for $\lambda = a+bi$ is paired with the conjugate block $J_k(\bar\lambda)$ of size $k$ for $\bar\lambda = a - bi$. Together they form a $2k \times 2k$ real matrix. Identify $\mathbb{R}^2 \cong \mathbb{C}$ via $(x,y) \leftrightarrow x + iy$. Multiplication by $\lambda = a+bi$ on $\mathbb{C}$ corresponds to the real linear map on $\mathbb{R}^2$ given by the matrix

$$C(a,b) = \begin{pmatrix} a & -b \\ b & a \end{pmatrix}.$$

The pair of complex Jordan blocks $J_k(\lambda), J_k(\bar\lambda)$ is similar over $\mathbb{R}$ to the **real Jordan block**

$$\mathcal{J}_k(a,b) = \begin{pmatrix} C(a,b) & I_2 & & \\ & C(a,b) & I_2 & \\ & & \ddots & I_2 \\ & & & C(a,b) \end{pmatrix} \in M_{2k}(\mathbb{R}),$$

a $2k \times 2k$ block matrix with $C(a,b)$ on the diagonal and $I_2$ on the superdiagonal.

### The theorem

**Real Jordan Canonical Form Theorem.** *Every $A \in M_n(\mathbb{R})$ is similar over $\mathbb{R}$ to a block-diagonal matrix*

$$A \sim_{\mathbb{R}} \bigoplus_i \mathcal{J}_{k_i}(\lambda_i) \;\oplus\; \bigoplus_j \mathcal{J}_{\ell_j}(a_j, b_j),$$

*where $\lambda_i \in \mathbb{R}$ are real eigenvalues, $(a_j, b_j) \in \mathbb{R} \times \mathbb{R}_{>0}$ parametrize complex conjugate pairs $a_j \pm i b_j$, and the total dimension satisfies*

$$\sum_i k_i + 2\sum_j \ell_j = n.$$

*This form is unique up to permutation of the blocks. Two real matrices are similar over $\mathbb{R}$ if and only if they have the same real Jordan form (up to block reordering).*

*Proof sketch of the theorem.* Over $\mathbb{C}$, $A$ has a Jordan form with blocks $J_k(\lambda)$. The real blocks $J_k(\lambda)$ for $\lambda \in \mathbb{R}$ are kept as-is. For each complex pair $a \pm bi$ ($b>0$), the blocks $J_k(a+bi)$ and $J_k(a-bi)$ appear with equal multiplicity (because the characteristic polynomial has real coefficients, so the Jordan structure is invariant under conjugation). The real and imaginary parts of the complex Jordan basis vectors, reordered as $(\operatorname{Re} v_1, \operatorname{Im} v_1, \operatorname{Re} v_2, \operatorname{Im} v_2, \dots)$, yield a real basis in which the action is exactly $\mathcal{J}_k(a,b)$. Uniqueness follows from uniqueness of the complex Jordan form together with the bijection between complex block-pairs and real blocks. $\square$

---

## Classification of Conjugacy Classes in $GL(n,\mathbb{R})$

A matrix $A \in M_n(\mathbb{R})$ lies in $GL(n,\mathbb{R})$ iff $\det A \neq 0$, i.e., **no eigenvalue is zero**. In terms of the real Jordan form:

- Every real eigenvalue $\lambda_i$ satisfies $\lambda_i \neq 0$.
- Every complex pair $(a_j, b_j)$ satisfies $a_j^2 + b_j^2 \neq 0$, i.e., $(a_j, b_j) \neq (0,0)$.

**Theorem.** *The conjugacy classes of $GL(n, \mathbb{R})$ are in bijection with the set of all real Jordan forms*

$$\bigoplus_{i=1}^{r} \mathcal{J}_{k_i}(\lambda_i) \;\oplus\; \bigoplus_{j=1}^{s} \mathcal{J}_{\ell_j}(a_j, b_j)$$

*satisfying:*

1. *(Real blocks)* $\lambda_i \in \mathbb{R}^\times = \mathbb{R} \setminus \{0\}$, $k_i \geq 1$;
2. *(Complex blocks)* $a_j \in \mathbb{R}$, $b_j \in \mathbb{R}_{> 0}$, $(a_j, b_j) \neq (0,0)$, $\ell_j \geq 1$;
3. *(Dimension)* $\displaystyle\sum_{i=1}^{r} k_i + 2\sum_{j=1}^{s} \ell_j = n$.

*Two elements of $GL(n,\mathbb{R})$ are conjugate if and only if their real Jordan forms agree up to permutation of blocks, i.e., if and only if they have the same multiset of real Jordan blocks $\{(\lambda_i, k_i)\}$ and the same multiset of complex Jordan blocks $\{(a_j, b_j, \ell_j)\}$.*

**Proof.** Conjugacy in $GL(n,\mathbb{R})$ is real similarity. By the real Jordan canonical form theorem, two real matrices are similar over $\mathbb{R}$ iff they share the same real Jordan form up to block reordering. Restricting to invertible matrices imposes $\lambda_i \neq 0$ and $(a_j,b_j) \neq (0,0)$. The dimension constraint is automatic from being $n \times n$. Conversely, every such formal real Jordan form is an invertible real $n \times n$ matrix (its determinant is $\prod \lambda_i^{k_i} \cdot \prod (a_j^2+b_j^2)^{\ell_j} \neq 0$), hence an element of $GL(n,\mathbb{R})$. $\square$

---

## Explicit Description of the Invariants

Equivalently, a conjugacy class is completely determined by the following data:

- A finite multiset of pairs $(\lambda, k)$ with $\lambda \in \mathbb{R}^\times$, $k \in \mathbb{Z}_{\geq 1}$ — these are the **real Jordan blocks** $\mathcal{J}_k(\lambda)$, each contributing dimension $k$.
- A finite multiset of triples $(a, b, \ell)$ with $a \in \mathbb{R}$, $b \in \mathbb{R}_{>0}$, $(a,b) \neq (0,0)$, $\ell \in \mathbb{Z}_{\geq 1}$ — these are the **complex Jordan blocks** $\mathcal{J}_\ell(a,b)$, each contributing dimension $2\ell$.
- The constraint $\sum k + 2\sum \ell = n$.

This data is equivalent to specifying, for the complexification $A_\mathbb{C}$: the Jordan block sizes for each nonzero real eigenvalue, and the Jordan block sizes for each non-real conjugate pair $a \pm bi$ ($b > 0$, $(a,b) \neq (0,0)$), subject to the dimension constraint.

---

## Summary

The conjugacy classes of $GL(n, \mathbb{R})$ are classified by the **real Jordan canonical form**. Each class corresponds uniquely to a multiset of invertible real Jordan blocks $\mathcal{J}_k(\lambda)$ ($\lambda \in \mathbb{R}^\times$) and invertible complex Jordan blocks $\mathcal{J}_\ell(a,b)$ ($a \in \mathbb{R}$, $b > 0$, $a^2+b^2 \neq 0$) whose total dimension sums to $n$. Two matrices in $GL(n,\mathbb{R})$ are conjugate if and only if they have identical real Jordan forms up to reordering of blocks.

